1
00:03:33,599 --> 00:03:39,599
Where your nightmares end...

2
00:07:07,199 --> 00:07:13,199
Eraserhead is a horror, drama film directed by David Lynch.

3
00:10:40,799 --> 00:10:46,799
First-time father Henry Spencer tries to survive his industrial environment, his angry
girlfriend, and the unbearable screams of his newly born mutant child.

4
00:14:14,399 --> 00:14:20,399
Henry Spencer, played by Jack Nance.

5
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Mary X, played by Charlotte Stewart.

6
00:21:21,599 --> 00:21:27,599
Mr. X, played by Allen Joseph.

7
00:24:55,200 --> 00:25:01,200
Mrs. X, played by Jeanne Bates.

8
00:28:28,799 --> 00:28:34,799
Beautiful Girl Across the Hall, played by Judith Roberts.

9
00:32:02,399 --> 00:32:08,399
Lady in the Radiator, played by Laurel Near.

10
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:42,000
Eraserhead is a 1977 American body horror film written, directed, produced, and
edited by David Lynch in his feature-length directorial debut.

11
00:39:09,599 --> 00:39:15,599
Lynch also created its score and sound design, which
included pieces by a variety of other musicians.

12
00:42:43,199 --> 00:42:49,199
Shot in black and white and surrealist style, the independent film was
Lynch's first feature-length effort following several short films.

13
00:46:16,799 --> 00:46:22,799
Starring Jack Nance, Charlotte Stewart, Jeanne Bates, Judith Anna Roberts, Laurel Near, and Jack Fisk, it tells the story
of a man (Nance) who is left to care for his grossly deformed child in a desolate industrial landscape.

14
00:49:50,400 --> 00:49:56,400
Eraserhead was produced with the assistance of the American Film
Institute (AFI) during Lynch's time studying there.

15
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:30,000
It nonetheless spent several years in principal photography because of funding difficulties; donations from Fisk and
his wife Sissy Spacek as well as Nance's wife and crew member Catherine Coulson kept production afloat.

16
00:56:57,599 --> 00:57:03,599
It was shot on several locations owned by the AFI in California, including
Greystone Mansion, and a set of disused stables in which Lynch lived.

17
01:00:31,199 --> 01:00:37,199
Lynch and sound designer Alan Splet spent a year working on the
film's audio after their studio was soundproofed.

18
01:04:04,799 --> 01:04:10,799
The soundtrack features organ music by Fats Waller and includes the song "In Heaven",
written and performed for the film by Peter Ivers, with lyrics by Lynch.

19
01:07:38,400 --> 01:07:44,400
Initially opening to small audiences and little interest, Eraserhead
gained popularity over several long runs as a midnight movie.

20
01:11:12,000 --> 01:11:18,000
Since its release, it has received widespread acclaim from
critics and has been considered a cult film.

21
01:14:45,599 --> 01:14:51,599
Its surrealist imagery and sexual undercurrents have been seen as key thematic
elements, and its intricate sound design as its technical highlight.

22
01:18:19,199 --> 01:18:25,199
In 2004, the film was selected by the Library of Congress for preservation in the United States
National Film Registry as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

23
01:21:52,800 --> 01:21:58,800
The film explores themes of baby, nightmare,
mutant, claustrophobia, parents-in-law.

24
01:25:26,399 --> 01:25:32,399
The tone is dark and emotional and mind_bending.
